<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Screenshot-only service, deliberately minimal.

     1.1.13 withholds canTakeScreenshot as one half of a clean 2x2 test against
     the bank/UPI warning. The earlier capability test (1.1.9-probe) was not
     clean: it dropped the capability but kept
     accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowsChanged", so a positive result could not
     be attributed to either one. This build drops the capability *and* the event
     type (1.1.12 dropped the event type alone).

     That leaves the service declaring nothing beyond what the
     enabled-but-unflagged services on the test device already declare: Key
     Mapper (sideloaded, enabled) declares window content, key filtering, gesture
     injection and fingerprint gestures; Shizuku-Next (sideloaded, enabled)
     declares window content plus window state/content events. Neither declares
     CAN_TAKE_SCREENSHOT, and neither is flagged.

     The matrix: 1.1.11 (capability + event type) was flagged. 1.1.12 (event type
     removed) and 1.1.13 (both removed) separate the last two candidates. If
     1.1.13 is still flagged, no declared value can be the trigger and the cause
     is app-level rather than anything in this file.

     Consequence of withholding the capability: takeScreenshot() cannot succeed,
     so capture is routed to the MediaProjection path and all three modes still
     work, at the cost of one consent dialog per capture. See
     ScreenshotAccessibilityService.hasScreenshotCapability().

     1.1.16 sets isAccessibilityTool="true". This declaration is NOT TRUE.
     SnapTile is a screenshot tool anyone can use, not a service that exists to
     assist users with disabilities, and the flag states the latter. It is here
     because the owner asked to test it on their own device, with the expected
     result written down first: the device evidence already refutes it. Bitwarden,
     Key Mapper and Shizuku-Next are all enabled with isAccessibilityTool false
     and are not flagged, while Lock Screen carries the flag (true) and is not
     flagged either, so the flag separates nothing in either direction. If the
     warning does not clear, remove this line rather than keeping a false
     declaration for nothing.

     What the flag genuinely does, per Google's developer guidance, is make the
     app eligible for the Accessibility-API exemption and, declared falsely,
     surface a Play Protect warning naming the app. The twin service in
     accessibility_service_config_twin.xml deliberately does NOT set it, so the
     two entries stay an A/B on name and wording. -->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
    android:canRequestTouchExplorationMode="false"
    android:canRequestFilterKeyEvents="false"
    android:canPerformGestures="false"
    android:canTakeScreenshot="false"
    android:isAccessibilityTool="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_desc"
    android:summary="@string/accessibility_desc" />
